```java
interface SplitStrategy {
    List<Split> computeSplits(long amountInCents, List<User> participants, Map<User, Object> params);
}

class EqualSplitStrategy implements SplitStrategy {
    public List<Split> computeSplits(long amountInCents, List<User> participants, Map<User, Object> params) {
        return splitEqually(amountInCents, participants);
    }
}

class ExactSplitStrategy implements SplitStrategy {
    public List<Split> computeSplits(long amountInCents, List<User> participants, Map<User, Object> amounts) {
        long sum = amounts.values().stream().mapToLong(v -> (Long) v).sum();
        if (sum != amountInCents)
            throw new InvalidSplitException("exact amounts must sum to " + amountInCents);
        return participants.stream()
            .map(u -> new Split(u, (Long) amounts.get(u)))
            .toList();
    }
}
```

Validation lives inside each strategy because the rule genuinely differs per type — exact-amount validates a sum, percentage validates against 100, shares can't fail validation at all since any positive share count is valid.

```java
List<Split> splitEqually(long amountInCents, List<User> participants) {
    int n = participants.size();
    long base = amountInCents / n;       // integer division, floor
    long remainder = amountInCents % n;  // 0 <= remainder < n

    List<Split> splits = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        long share = base + (i < remainder ? 1 : 0);
        splits.add(new Split(participants.get(i), share));
    }
    return splits;
}

// splitEqually(1000, [alice, bob, carol]) -> base=333, remainder=1
// alice=334, bob=333, carol=333  ->  sum = 1000 exactly
```

The first `remainder` participants (by list order, which callers must keep stable) get one extra cent each. `base * n + remainder == amountInCents` always holds, so the splits sum to the original total with no floating-point step anywhere.

```java
List<Transaction> simplifyDebts(Map<User, Long> netBalanceCents) {
    PriorityQueue<Balance> creditors =
        new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Long.compare(b.amount, a.amount));
    PriorityQueue<Balance> debtors =
        new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> Long.compare(b.amount, a.amount));

    for (var e : netBalanceCents.entrySet()) {
        if (e.getValue() > 0) creditors.add(new Balance(e.getKey(), e.getValue()));
        else if (e.getValue() < 0) debtors.add(new Balance(e.getKey(), -e.getValue()));
    }

    List<Transaction> settlements = new ArrayList<>();
    while (!creditors.isEmpty() && !debtors.isEmpty()) {
        Balance c = creditors.poll();
        Balance d = debtors.poll();
        long settle = Math.min(c.amount, d.amount);
        settlements.add(new Transaction(d.user, c.user, settle));
        c.amount -= settle;
        d.amount -= settle;
        if (c.amount > 0) creditors.add(c);
        if (d.amount > 0) debtors.add(d);
    }
    return settlements;
}
```

Each round matches the largest creditor with the largest debtor and settles the smaller of the two amounts, so every round fully zeroes out at least one person. That bounds the result at `n - 1` transactions for `n` people with a nonzero balance, and runs in `O(n log n)`.
